INSERT INTO public.qb_questions (subject_id, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, points, tags) VALUES
('9dc3ade3-26ea-41fa-a83f-95a7440889b8', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'A cotton mixing has high micronaire (5.5) but relatively short staple length (26mm). What spinning problem is this combination most likely to cause?', '["No issues at all, since micronaire and length are unrelated to spinnability","Coarser, less mature-feeling yarn with acceptable strength but a higher risk of neps due to the mismatch between fiber fineness expectations and length","Automatically the best possible spinning quality","Complete inability to spin any yarn count"]'::jsonb, 'Coarser, less mature-feeling yarn with acceptable strength but a higher risk of neps due to the mismatch between fiber fineness expectations and length', 'High micronaire (coarser/more mature fiber) combined with short staple length is a suboptimal combination for fine counts — the short length limits achievable fineness/strength while the coarseness increases the risk of neps and reduces yarn smoothness, a classic fiber-selection tradeoff.', 3, '{"fiber-properties","micronaire"}'::text[]),
('9dc3ade3-26ea-41fa-a83f-95a7440889b8', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'In the blow room, the transition from aggressive to progressively gentler opening/cleaning action across successive machines exists mainly because:', '["Later machines are simply older and weaker","Early, coarser cleaning removes large trash without excessive fiber damage, while later stages must be gentler since the tuft size is already smaller and more fiber damage/nep formation risk increases with aggressive action on small tufts","It has no technical reason, just historical convention","Later machines only clean synthetic fiber, not cotton"]'::jsonb, 'Early, coarser cleaning removes large trash without excessive fiber damage, while later stages must be gentler since the tuft size is already smaller and more fiber damage/nep formation risk increases with aggressive action on small tufts', 'Blow room design follows a ''clean at the earliest, gentlest point possible'' principle — early aggressive beating removes large impurities from big tufts efficiently, but by the later stages, tufts are smaller and more fragile, so gentler action is used to avoid excessive neps and fiber breakage.', 3, '{"blowroom"}'::text[]),
('9dc3ade3-26ea-41fa-a83f-95a7440889b8', 'intermediate', 'multiple_choice', 'Why does carding intentionally allow a small percentage of ''card waste'' (noils/fly) rather than trying to achieve zero fiber loss?', '["It is impossible to avoid ANY loss, and zero loss is not the actual goal — some loss of very short fibers, neps, and remaining trash is necessary to achieve the cleaning/quality level needed downstream","Card waste has no effect on final yarn quality either way","The goal is actually to maximize waste, not minimize it","Modern cards produce zero waste by design"]'::jsonb, 'It is impossible to avoid ANY loss, and zero loss is not the actual goal — some loss of very short fibers, neps, and remaining trash is necessary to achieve the cleaning/quality level needed downstream', 'Carding is deliberately tuned to remove short fibers/neps/remaining trash that would otherwise degrade yarn evenness and strength; mills balance the waste percentage against the yarn quality requirement, rather than chasing an unrealistic zero-waste target.', 2, '{"carding","waste"}'::text[]),
('9dc3ade3-26ea-41fa-a83f-95a7440889b8', 'basic', 'multiple_choice', 'The carding machine is often called the ''heart of spinning'' primarily because it is the stage where:', '["Yarn twist is first inserted","Fibers are individualized (separated from tufts into single fibers), most remaining trash is removed, and the first continuous strand (sliver) is formed","The final yarn count is set","Fibers are dyed for the first time"]'::jsonb, 'Fibers are individualized (separated from tufts into single fibers), most remaining trash is removed, and the first continuous strand (sliver) is formed', 'No later process can fully compensate for poor carding — it is the critical stage that converts tangled tufts into individualized fibers formed into sliver, which is why quality problems from carding tend to persist through the rest of the spinning process.', 1, '{"carding"}'::text[]),
('9dc3ade3-26ea-41fa-a83f-95a7440889b8', 'intermediate', 'multiple_choice', '''Doubling'' at the draw frame — combining multiple slivers (e.g., 6 or 8) into one — primarily improves yarn quality by:', '["Making the final yarn count coarser regardless of draft","Averaging out irregularities between individual slivers, since random unevenness in one sliver tends to be compensated by the others, improving overall evenness","Removing all remaining trash particles","Inserting twist into the sliver"]'::jsonb, 'Averaging out irregularities between individual slivers, since random unevenness in one sliver tends to be compensated by the others, improving overall evenness', 'This is a statistical averaging effect: if one sliver happens to be thick at a point, it''s unlikely all the doubled slivers are thick at exactly the same point, so combining and then drafting them back down evens out the irregularities — a core principle explaining why draw frame passages improve sliver/yarn evenness.', 2, '{"draw-frame","doubling"}'::text[]),
('9dc3ade3-26ea-41fa-a83f-95a7440889b8', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'If draw frame doubling improves evenness through averaging, why don''t mills use an extremely high number of doublings (e.g., 20+) at every passage to maximize this benefit?', '["Higher doubling numbers have literally zero downside","Beyond a certain point, diminishing returns set in, and excessive doubling with correspondingly high draft can reintroduce irregularity (drafting waves) and slow the process, so mills optimize doubling/draft against real machine and quality constraints","The rule is arbitrary and unrelated to any technical constraint","It is physically impossible to double more than 8 slivers"]'::jsonb, 'Beyond a certain point, diminishing returns set in, and excessive doubling with correspondingly high draft can reintroduce irregularity (drafting waves) and slow the process, so mills optimize doubling/draft against real machine and quality constraints', 'Every doubling must be matched with a corresponding draft to bring the combined sliver back to target weight; very high draft levels risk their own irregularity (drafting wave defects) from imperfect fiber control, so real mills balance doubling number against draft capability rather than maximizing doubling alone.', 3, '{"draw-frame","draft"}'::text[]),
('9dc3ade3-26ea-41fa-a83f-95a7440889b8', 'intermediate', 'multiple_choice', 'The roving frame (simplex/speed frame) inserts a small amount of ''false'' twist into the roving mainly to:', '["Give the roving its final, permanent yarn twist","Give the roving enough cohesion/strength to survive handling and unwinding at the next stage (ring frame) without breaking, since the fiber strand is still thick and weak at this stage","Add color to the roving","Reduce the roving''s linear density to final yarn count immediately"]'::jsonb, 'Give the roving enough cohesion/strength to survive handling and unwinding at the next stage (ring frame) without breaking, since the fiber strand is still thick and weak at this stage', 'Roving twist is deliberately kept low (much lower than final yarn twist) — just enough to hold the still-thick, low-strength strand together for winding onto bobbins and subsequent unwinding at the ring frame, since the roving will be drafted down much further before final twist is applied.', 2, '{"roving","speed-frame"}'::text[]),
('9dc3ade3-26ea-41fa-a83f-95a7440889b8', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'In ring spinning, the traveller (the small C-shaped clip riding on the ring) primarily functions to:', '["Cut the yarn to length","Guide the yarn while rotating around the ring slower than the spindle, and this speed difference is what actually inserts twist into the yarn while winding it onto the bobbin","Clean the yarn of trash","Measure the final yarn count"]'::jsonb, 'Guide the yarn while rotating around the ring slower than the spindle, and this speed difference is what actually inserts twist into the yarn while winding it onto the bobbin', 'The traveller is dragged around the ring by the yarn itself, always lagging slightly behind the spindle''s rotation speed due to friction/drag — this rotational speed difference between spindle and traveller is exactly what inserts twist into the yarn as it winds onto the bobbin.', 3, '{"ring-spinning","traveller"}'::text[]),
('9dc3ade3-26ea-41fa-a83f-95a7440889b8', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'A ring frame is running with excessive end breakage. Increasing the traveller weight (heavier traveller) is sometimes used as a corrective action because it:', '["Always increases end breakage further, making the problem worse","Reduces yarn tension by allowing the traveller to lag more, which can help reduce breakage caused by excessive tension, though at the cost of reduced twist per given spindle speed","Has no relationship to yarn tension at all","Automatically increases the final yarn twist multiplier"]'::jsonb, 'Reduces yarn tension by allowing the traveller to lag more, which can help reduce breakage caused by excessive tension, though at the cost of reduced twist per given spindle speed', 'Traveller weight is a key tension-control variable: a heavier traveller lags further behind the spindle (more drag), reducing balloon tension on the yarn, which can cut breakage from over-tensioning — but the tradeoff is that for the same conditions it can also affect twist insertion efficiency and requires careful selection against yarn count and machine speed.', 3, '{"ring-spinning","traveller","end-breakage"}'::text[]),
('9dc3ade3-26ea-41fa-a83f-95a7440889b8', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'Twist Multiplier (TM) / Twist Factor is used instead of a fixed TPI (turns per inch) value across different yarn counts because:', '["TM is a completely different, unrelated property with no formula connection to TPI","TPI must scale with yarn count (finer yarns need proportionally more turns per inch for equivalent yarn character/strength), and TM (TPI divided by the square root of the count) gives a count-independent way to specify twist ''level'' consistently across different counts","TM only applies to synthetic yarns, not cotton","TM measures yarn strength directly, not twist"]'::jsonb, 'TPI must scale with yarn count (finer yarns need proportionally more turns per inch for equivalent yarn character/strength), and TM (TPI divided by the square root of the count) gives a count-independent way to specify twist ''level'' consistently across different counts', 'The formula TPI = TM × √(count) reflects that finer yarns naturally require more twists per inch to achieve comparable structural cohesion; TM strips out this count-dependence, letting spinners specify ''soft twist'' vs ''hard twist'' consistently regardless of the actual count being spun.', 3, '{"twist-multiplier"}'::text[]),
('9dc3ade3-26ea-41fa-a83f-95a7440889b8', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'Increasing twist multiplier (harder twist) in a ring-spun yarn generally increases yarn strength up to a point, but beyond an optimum TM, strength actually starts to DECREASE. Why?', '["Higher twist always increases strength with no upper limit","Beyond the optimum twist angle, fibers become oriented too obliquely to the yarn axis, reducing their effective contribution to axial (lengthwise) strength even though total fiber cohesion/friction increases","Twist has no effect on yarn strength at any level","It only happens with synthetic fiber, never cotton"]'::jsonb, 'Beyond the optimum twist angle, fibers become oriented too obliquely to the yarn axis, reducing their effective contribution to axial (lengthwise) strength even though total fiber cohesion/friction increases', 'Yarn strength depends on fiber orientation relative to the yarn axis; moderate twist improves fiber cohesion and strength, but excessive twist angles the fibers too far from the yarn''s lengthwise axis, so their individual tensile strength contributes less to the yarn''s overall axial strength — this is the well-known ''strength vs twist'' optimum curve in spinning theory.', 3, '{"twist","yarn-strength"}'::text[]),
('9dc3ade3-26ea-41fa-a83f-95a7440889b8', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'Rotor (open-end) spinning produces yarn through a fundamentally different mechanism than ring spinning. The key structural difference in the resulting yarn is that rotor yarn typically has:', '["Identical structure and properties to ring-spun yarn in every way","A bulkier, less compact structure with characteristic ''wrapper fibers'' around a twisted core, generally giving lower tenacity but often better evenness at coarser counts","Always higher strength than ring-spun yarn at every count","No twist at all, unlike ring yarn"]'::jsonb, 'A bulkier, less compact structure with characteristic ''wrapper fibers'' around a twisted core, generally giving lower tenacity but often better evenness at coarser counts', 'Rotor spinning builds yarn by peeling fiber from a rotor groove, resulting in a core of twisted fibers wrapped by some loosely-bound ''wrapper'' fibers — this gives a bulkier, more voluminous yarn with generally lower strength than ring yarn of the same count, but with process advantages like fewer ends down and good evenness.', 3, '{"rotor-spinning","comparison"}'::text[]),
('9dc3ade3-26ea-41fa-a83f-95a7440889b8', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'Rotor spinning can process fiber directly from sliver (skipping the roving frame stage entirely) mainly because:', '["Rotor spinning cannot achieve any significant draft, so no roving is needed","The rotor spinning process itself achieves very high total draft in a single stage via the opening roller and rotor mechanism, eliminating the need for the intermediate roving step required by ring spinning''s lower per-stage draft capability","Roving frames are physically incompatible with rotor machines for unrelated mechanical reasons","Rotor yarn does not require any drafting at all"]'::jsonb, 'The rotor spinning process itself achieves very high total draft in a single stage via the opening roller and rotor mechanism, eliminating the need for the intermediate roving step required by ring spinning''s lower per-stage draft capability', 'A key process economy of rotor spinning is its very high achievable draft (fiber individualization by the opening roller, then reassembly in the rotor), allowing it to go directly from draw-frame sliver to finished yarn — this eliminates the roving frame and associated bobbin handling that ring spinning requires.', 3, '{"rotor-spinning","process-economy"}'::text[]),
('9dc3ade3-26ea-41fa-a83f-95a7440889b8', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'Air-jet spinning creates yarn using high-velocity compressed air vortices to wrap fibers around a core, rather than mechanical twist insertion. This method''s main commercial advantage is:', '["It is the only method capable of producing any yarn at all","Very high production speed (much faster than ring spinning) since there''s no traveller/spindle rotational speed limit","It always produces stronger yarn than ring spinning at every count","It eliminates the need for any drafting"]'::jsonb, 'Very high production speed (much faster than ring spinning) since there''s no traveller/spindle rotational speed limit', 'Because air-jet spinning doesn''t rely on a physically rotating traveller/spindle (a major mechanical speed bottleneck in ring spinning), it can run at dramatically higher linear production speeds — its tradeoff is a yarn structure/hand-feel and strength profile different from (often somewhat inferior in strength to) ring-spun yarn.', 3, '{"air-jet-spinning"}'::text[]),
('9dc3ade3-26ea-41fa-a83f-95a7440889b8', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'Compact spinning (with a condensing/compacting zone added after the front roller of a ring frame) primarily improves yarn quality by:', '["Doubling the machine''s production speed","Reducing the ''spinning triangle'' width, which decreases hairiness and improves strength/evenness by better controlling fiber edges before twist insertion","Eliminating the need for any twist","Making the yarn significantly coarser"]'::jsonb, 'Reducing the ''spinning triangle'' width, which decreases hairiness and improves strength/evenness by better controlling fiber edges before twist insertion', 'In conventional ring spinning, the ''spinning triangle'' (where drafted fiber strand converges before twist) leaves edge fibers poorly bound, causing hairiness; compact spinning condenses the strand width just before twisting, tightly controlling fiber edges and significantly reducing hairiness while improving strength.', 3, '{"compact-spinning"}'::text[]),
('9dc3ade3-26ea-41fa-a83f-95a7440889b8', 'intermediate', 'multiple_choice', 'USTER statistics (a globally recognized benchmarking system) are used in spinning quality control primarily to:', '["Set the final selling price of yarn directly","Compare a mill''s yarn evenness, imperfections (thin/thick places, neps), and hairiness against percentile benchmarks from mills worldwide, for objective quality positioning","Measure only the fiber''s raw cotton grade before spinning","Replace the need for any physical yarn testing"]'::jsonb, 'Compare a mill''s yarn evenness, imperfections (thin/thick places, neps), and hairiness against percentile benchmarks from mills worldwide, for objective quality positioning', 'USTER Statistics give percentile tables (e.g., ''USTER 25%'' meaning better than 75% of mills worldwide) for yarn evenness (CV%), imperfections, and hairiness, letting mills objectively benchmark their own yarn quality against a global reference rather than relying on internal-only comparisons.', 2, '{"USTER","quality-benchmark"}'::text[]),
('9dc3ade3-26ea-41fa-a83f-95a7440889b8', 'basic', 'multiple_choice', 'Yarn evenness is commonly expressed as ''CV%'' (coefficient of variation). A LOWER CV% indicates:', '["A worse, more uneven yarn","A better, more even/uniform yarn, since CV% measures the relative variability of yarn mass along its length","Has no relationship to yarn quality at all","Only relates to yarn color consistency"]'::jsonb, 'A better, more even/uniform yarn, since CV% measures the relative variability of yarn mass along its length', 'CV% (standard deviation of mass variation divided by mean mass, as a percentage) quantifies how much the yarn''s linear density fluctuates along its length — lower CV% means more consistent mass distribution, i.e., a more even yarn, generally correlating with fewer visible defects and better downstream performance.', 1, '{"CV-percent","evenness"}'::text[]),
('9dc3ade3-26ea-41fa-a83f-95a7440889b8', 'basic', 'multiple_choice', '''Neps'' in spinning are defined as:', '["Long, straight fiber sections with no entanglement","Small, tightly tangled knots of fiber that appear as visible faults in yarn/fabric and disrupt evenness and dye uptake","A measure of the yarn''s twist level","The technical term for the yarn''s final count"]'::jsonb, 'Small, tightly tangled knots of fiber that appear as visible faults in yarn/fabric and disrupt evenness and dye uptake', 'Neps are small entangled fiber knots that form during processing (especially from immature/short fibers or excessive mechanical action); they show up as visible specks in yarn and dyed fabric, and are heavily tracked as a key quality metric across carding through spinning.', 1, '{"neps"}'::text[]),
('9dc3ade3-26ea-41fa-a83f-95a7440889b8', 'basic', 'multiple_choice', 'Combing (in combed yarn production) is an extra process added after carding specifically to:', '["Add extra twist to the sliver","Remove short fibers below a set length (noils) and further straighten/parallelize the remaining longer fibers, giving finer, stronger, smoother yarn than carded-only yarn","Dye the fiber before spinning","Reduce the sliver''s overall fiber content to zero"]'::jsonb, 'Remove short fibers below a set length (noils) and further straighten/parallelize the remaining longer fibers, giving finer, stronger, smoother yarn than carded-only yarn', 'Combing removes a percentage of the shortest fibers (noils, often 12-18%) and aligns the remaining longer fibers more parallel to each other, which allows finer, stronger, and smoother ''combed'' yarn compared to carded yarn made from the same raw cotton.', 1, '{"combing"}'::text[]),
('9dc3ade3-26ea-41fa-a83f-95a7440889b8', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'A mill decides to increase the noil percentage removed during combing from 12% to 18% for the same raw cotton. What is the most likely tradeoff?', '["No tradeoff at all — pure quality improvement with zero cost implication","Improved yarn quality (fewer short fibers, better evenness/strength) but higher raw material cost per kg of finished yarn, since more usable fiber is discarded as noil","Decreased yarn quality with higher cost","It has no effect on either cost or quality"]'::jsonb, 'Improved yarn quality (fewer short fibers, better evenness/strength) but higher raw material cost per kg of finished yarn, since more usable fiber is discarded as noil', 'Higher noil extraction removes more short fiber, generally improving the spun yarn''s evenness, strength, and smoothness — but since noil is a byproduct sold at lower value than the main yarn, discarding more of it as noil directly raises the effective raw material cost per kg of finished combed yarn.', 3, '{"combing","cost-quality-tradeoff"}'::text[]),
('9dc3ade3-26ea-41fa-a83f-95a7440889b8', 'intermediate', 'multiple_choice', 'Two spinning mills use identical raw cotton, but Mill A produces combed yarn while Mill B produces carded yarn at the same count. Mill A''s yarn will typically show:', '["Identical CV%, strength, and hairiness to Mill B''s yarn","Better evenness, higher strength, and lower hairiness, but at a higher production cost due to the extra combing process and fiber waste (noil)","Worse quality in every metric compared to carded yarn","Combed and carded processes cannot use the same raw cotton"]'::jsonb, 'Better evenness, higher strength, and lower hairiness, but at a higher production cost due to the extra combing process and fiber waste (noil)', 'Combing''s short-fiber removal and fiber parallelization directly translate into measurably better yarn evenness, strength, and reduced hairiness versus carded yarn from the same cotton — this quality premium is exactly why combed yarn commands a higher price, reflecting its added process cost.', 2, '{"combed-vs-carded"}'::text[]),
('9dc3ade3-26ea-41fa-a83f-95a7440889b8', 'intermediate', 'multiple_choice', 'Yarn count systems are broadly split into ''direct'' (e.g., Tex, Denier — mass per fixed length) and ''indirect'' (e.g., English Count Ne, Metric Count Nm — length per fixed mass) systems. In an INDIRECT system, a HIGHER count number means:', '["A coarser (thicker) yarn","A finer (thinner) yarn, since more length is obtained from the same fixed unit of mass","Exactly the same thickness regardless of the number","The yarn''s twist level, not its thickness"]'::jsonb, 'A finer (thinner) yarn, since more length is obtained from the same fixed unit of mass', 'Indirect systems (Ne, Nm) are length-per-mass — a higher number means more length per unit weight, which only happens if the yarn is thinner (finer); this is the opposite relationship from direct systems (Tex, Denier) where a higher number means a coarser yarn (more mass per fixed length).', 2, '{"yarn-count-systems"}'::text[]),
('9dc3ade3-26ea-41fa-a83f-95a7440889b8', 'intermediate', 'multiple_choice', 'A yarn is described as ''2/40s Ne''. This notation means:', '["A single 40s count yarn with no plying","Two single yarns of 40s English count each, twisted (plied) together, giving a resultant plied yarn roughly half as fine (coarser) as either single","A yarn that changes count every 2 meters","40 individual yarns twisted together"]'::jsonb, 'Two single yarns of 40s English count each, twisted (plied) together, giving a resultant plied yarn roughly half as fine (coarser) as either single', 'The ''2/'' prefix means two ends of 40s single yarn are plied together; since plying combines mass, the resultant yarn''s approximate count follows 1/resultant = 1/40 + 1/40, giving roughly 20s — notably coarser than either individual single, a common point of confusion for beginners.', 2, '{"yarn-count","plying"}'::text[]),
('9dc3ade3-26ea-41fa-a83f-95a7440889b8', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'Doubling (plying) two single yarns together generally gives a stronger resultant yarn than a single yarn of the same final linear density (count). This is mainly because:', '["Plying has no effect on strength, only appearance","Twisting two independently-formed yarns together allows any localized weak spots in one single to be reinforced by the other single at that same point, since weak spots rarely coincide exactly at the same location in both","Plied yarn always has less total fiber than an equivalent single yarn","Plying removes all twist from the yarn"]'::jsonb, 'Twisting two independently-formed yarns together allows any localized weak spots in one single to be reinforced by the other single at that same point, since weak spots rarely coincide exactly at the same location in both', 'This mirrors the doubling-averaging principle from draw frame: since each single yarn''s weak points occur somewhat randomly along its length, it''s statistically unlikely both plied singles are weak at the exact same point simultaneously, so the stronger single ''carries'' that section — giving the plied yarn better overall strength consistency than an equivalent single yarn.', 3, '{"plying","yarn-strength"}'::text[]),
('9dc3ade3-26ea-41fa-a83f-95a7440889b8', 'basic', 'multiple_choice', '''S-twist'' and ''Z-twist'' refer to:', '["Two different yarn count systems","The direction of twist in a yarn (S = counter-clockwise spiral direction, Z = clockwise), visually matching the middle diagonal of the respective letter","Two grades of raw cotton fiber","A measure of yarn strength"]'::jsonb, 'The direction of twist in a yarn (S = counter-clockwise spiral direction, Z = clockwise), visually matching the middle diagonal of the respective letter', 'S and Z twist describe the visual direction the fiber spirals travel in the yarn — matching the diagonal stroke of the letters S or Z — and this direction matters for downstream processes like plying (plying direction is usually opposite to the singles'' twist direction for balance) and fabric appearance (e.g., crepe effects).', 1, '{"twist-direction"}'::text[]);
INSERT INTO public.qb_questions (subject_id, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, points, tags) VALUES
('9dc3ade3-26ea-41fa-a83f-95a7440889b8', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'When plying two S-twisted singles together, the ply twist is conventionally applied in the Z direction (opposite to the singles). What is the technical reason for this convention?', '["It is purely arbitrary with no technical basis","Opposing twist directions between singles and ply twist creates a balanced, stable yarn structure that resists excessive torque/snarling, whereas same-direction twisting would over-twist the singles and cause instability","Same-direction twisting is actually stronger and preferred whenever possible","It only matters for synthetic yarns, not cotton"]'::jsonb, 'Opposing twist directions between singles and ply twist creates a balanced, stable yarn structure that resists excessive torque/snarling, whereas same-direction twisting would over-twist the singles and cause instability', 'If ply twist were applied in the same direction as the singles'' twist, it would effectively add MORE twist to each single (risking over-twist, kinking, and snarling); opposite-direction ply twist instead partially untwists each single locally while binding them together, producing a more torque-balanced, stable, and better-handling plied yarn.', 3, '{"plying","twist-balance"}'::text[]),
('9dc3ade3-26ea-41fa-a83f-95a7440889b8', 'intermediate', 'multiple_choice', 'Winding (from ring bobbin to cone) includes automatic yarn clearers that detect and cut out defects like thick places, thin places, and foreign fiber contamination. This step exists mainly because:', '["It has no real quality purpose, just a packaging formality","Removing severe localized defects before the yarn reaches weaving/knitting prevents fabric-level faults (like thick bars or holes) that would be far more costly to find and fix after fabric or garment production","Winding is only for measuring the final yarn length, unrelated to quality","Ring bobbins never contain any defects, making clearers unnecessary"]'::jsonb, 'Removing severe localized defects before the yarn reaches weaving/knitting prevents fabric-level faults (like thick bars or holes) that would be far more costly to find and fix after fabric or garment production', 'Yarn clearers use optical/capacitive sensors to detect defects exceeding set thresholds and automatically cut-and-splice them out during winding — catching a defect here is vastly cheaper than the alternative of a defective yarn making it all the way into woven/knitted fabric or a finished garment.', 2, '{"winding","yarn-clearer"}'::text[]),
('9dc3ade3-26ea-41fa-a83f-95a7440889b8', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', '''Classimat'' or similar yarn fault classification systems categorize defects by both length and thickness/thinness. Why does length matter alongside the thick/thin severity?', '["Length has no bearing on how visible or problematic a defect is in fabric","A short, severe thick place may be less visually disruptive in fabric than a long, moderate thick place — so classification by both dimensions gives a more accurate picture of the fault''s real fabric-level impact","Only thickness matters; length classification is purely bureaucratic","Length only matters for measuring total yarn package weight"]'::jsonb, 'A short, severe thick place may be less visually disruptive in fabric than a long, moderate thick place — so classification by both dimensions gives a more accurate picture of the fault''s real fabric-level impact', 'Fault classification systems plot defects on a length-vs-thickness grid because both dimensions affect visibility and severity in the final fabric differently — a long thin defect can be as visually disruptive as a short thick one, so a two-dimensional classification better predicts real fabric quality impact than either metric alone.', 3, '{"yarn-faults","classification"}'::text[]),
('9dc3ade3-26ea-41fa-a83f-95a7440889b8', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'Foreign fiber contamination (e.g., polypropylene strands mixed into cotton) is a particularly serious spinning quality issue because:', '["It has no visible effect on the final fabric at all","These contaminant fibers often don''t take dye the same way as cotton, so they remain highly visible as white/off-color specks in dyed fabric, and they''re difficult to fully detect/remove during ginning or spinning","It only affects the yarn''s twist level","It makes the yarn stronger, so mills intentionally add it"]'::jsonb, 'These contaminant fibers often don''t take dye the same way as cotton, so they remain highly visible as white/off-color specks in dyed fabric, and they''re difficult to fully detect/remove during ginning or spinning', 'Because polypropylene and similar contaminants have completely different dye affinity than cotton, they stay conspicuously undyed/off-shade in finished dyed fabric even in tiny amounts, making foreign fiber a major quality/rejection risk that''s hard to fully eliminate through standard cleaning processes.', 3, '{"contamination","foreign-fiber"}'::text[]),
('9dc3ade3-26ea-41fa-a83f-95a7440889b8', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', '''Spinning limit'' (the finest count theoretically/practically spinnable from a given fiber) is primarily constrained by:', '["The color of the fiber only","The fiber''s length, fineness, and the minimum number of fibers needed in the yarn cross-section to maintain adequate cohesion/strength","The spinning machine''s brand name","The ambient room temperature alone"]'::jsonb, 'The fiber''s length, fineness, and the minimum number of fibers needed in the yarn cross-section to maintain adequate cohesion/strength', 'There''s a practical minimum number of fibers that must be present in any yarn cross-section for the fibers to grip each other via twist and friction; finer/longer fiber allows a thinner yarn while still meeting this minimum fiber count, which is why fiber fineness and length directly set the spinning limit for a given raw material.', 3, '{"spinning-limit"}'::text[]),
('9dc3ade3-26ea-41fa-a83f-95a7440889b8', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'Why is fiber MATURITY (a measure of cell wall development, distinct from fineness) important in cotton spinning, even though micronaire already partly reflects it?', '["Maturity has zero effect on spinning or dyeing outcomes","Immature fibers (thin cell walls) are weaker, more prone to breaking and neps, and take dye differently, causing visible ''immature fiber'' specks even when average micronaire looks acceptable","Maturity only matters for synthetic fibers, not cotton","Higher maturity always means a finer, not coarser, fiber"]'::jsonb, 'Immature fibers (thin cell walls) are weaker, more prone to breaking and neps, and take dye differently, causing visible ''immature fiber'' specks even when average micronaire looks acceptable', 'Because micronaire is an average measure combining fineness and maturity, a mixing can have ''acceptable'' average micronaire while still containing a problematic proportion of individually immature fibers — these weak, thin-walled fibers break easily (causing neps) and dye unevenly, so maturity is tracked as a separate, important metric.', 3, '{"fiber-maturity"}'::text[]),
('9dc3ade3-26ea-41fa-a83f-95a7440889b8', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'A ring-spinning mill wants to increase production speed (spindle RPM) on an existing count without changing the yarn''s twist multiplier. What must necessarily change?', '["Nothing else needs to change","The traveller''s rotational speed (and hence the ring frame''s front roller delivery/draft speed) must increase proportionally to maintain the same twist level (turns per unit length) at the higher spindle speed","The yarn count must automatically become finer","Twist multiplier is entirely unrelated to spindle speed"]'::jsonb, 'The traveller''s rotational speed (and hence the ring frame''s front roller delivery/draft speed) must increase proportionally to maintain the same twist level (turns per unit length) at the higher spindle speed', 'Twist per unit length depends on the ratio of spindle rotation to the linear delivery speed of yarn from the front roller; to raise spindle RPM (production speed) while holding twist multiplier constant, the front roller delivery speed must increase proportionally, otherwise twist level would unintentionally increase.', 3, '{"ring-spinning","production-speed"}'::text[]),
('9dc3ade3-26ea-41fa-a83f-95a7440889b8', 'basic', 'multiple_choice', 'Which of the following is generally NOT a direct cause of increased end breakage at the ring frame?', '["Excessive spinning tension (e.g., wrong traveller weight for the count/speed)","Poor fiber quality (short, weak, or immature fibers)","Uneven roving or drafting irregularities feeding weak spots into the yarn","The brand of packaging used for the final cones"]'::jsonb, 'The brand of packaging used for the final cones', 'Cone packaging brand occurs after spinning is already complete and has no causal relationship to end breakage during ring spinning — the other three factors (tension/traveller selection, fiber quality, and upstream evenness) are all genuine, well-documented root causes of ring frame end breaks.', 1, '{"end-breakage"}'::text[]),
('9dc3ade3-26ea-41fa-a83f-95a7440889b8', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'A spinning mill is evaluating switching a portion of production from ring spinning to rotor spinning for a coarse count (e.g., 10s Ne) intended for denim/industrial fabric. The most compelling technical justification is:', '["Rotor yarn is always superior in every property to ring yarn","At coarse counts where the strength/hairiness disadvantage of rotor yarn matters less, rotor spinning''s higher production speed and ability to use lower-grade/waste fiber can give a significant cost advantage","Ring spinning cannot produce any coarse counts at all","Rotor spinning requires no raw fiber input"]'::jsonb, 'At coarse counts where the strength/hairiness disadvantage of rotor yarn matters less, rotor spinning''s higher production speed and ability to use lower-grade/waste fiber can give a significant cost advantage', 'Rotor spinning''s relative weaknesses (lower strength, bulkier structure) matter less for coarse, less-demanding end-uses like denim, while its strengths (higher production speed, tolerance for shorter/lower-grade fiber including recycled/waste content, no roving step) become a strong cost advantage — this is exactly why rotor spinning dominates in coarse-count denim-type yarn production.', 3, '{"rotor-vs-ring","process-selection"}'::text[]),
('9dc3ade3-26ea-41fa-a83f-95a7440889b8', 'intermediate', 'multiple_choice', 'Yarn hairiness (protruding fiber ends sticking out from the yarn''s main body) is generally considered undesirable mainly because it:', '["Always improves the yarn''s dyeability with no downside","Increases friction during weaving/knitting (more breakages, fluff generation), can cause a fuzzy fabric surface appearance, and contributes to pilling in the finished fabric","Has absolutely no relevance to fabric quality","Only matters for wool yarn, not cotton"]'::jsonb, 'Increases friction during weaving/knitting (more breakages, fluff generation), can cause a fuzzy fabric surface appearance, and contributes to pilling in the finished fabric', 'Protruding hairy fibers increase inter-yarn friction during fabric formation (raising breakage risk and machine fluff/lint buildup), and in the finished fabric, these loose fiber ends are exactly what tangle together to form pills — so hairiness is tracked as a key negative quality metric, especially for smooth-surface fabric requirements.', 2, '{"hairiness"}'::text[]),
('9dc3ade3-26ea-41fa-a83f-95a7440889b8', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'A yarn supplier claims their combed 30s Ne cotton yarn has ''zero imperfections.'' A quality manager should treat this claim as:', '["Fully credible, since combed cotton yarn theoretically can have zero defects","Highly suspect — real spun yarn, even high-quality combed yarn, always has a measurable (if very low) imperfection rate per USTER-style testing; ''zero'' is not a realistic claim for any staple-spun yarn","Irrelevant, since imperfections don''t matter for combed yarn","Only possible if the yarn is 100% polyester filament, which this is not"]'::jsonb, 'Highly suspect — real spun yarn, even high-quality combed yarn, always has a measurable (if very low) imperfection rate per USTER-style testing; ''zero'' is not a realistic claim for any staple-spun yarn', 'Because staple-fiber spinning (ring, rotor, air-jet) inherently involves discrete fiber ends and natural variability, some baseline level of thin places, thick places, and neps is unavoidable — USTER benchmarks report imperfection rates even for top-quality (5% percentile) yarns, so a genuine zero-imperfection claim should raise a quality manager''s skepticism about the testing methodology used.', 3, '{"quality-claims","imperfections"}'::text[]),
('9dc3ade3-26ea-41fa-a83f-95a7440889b8', 'intermediate', 'multiple_choice', 'In a cotton mixing (blend) plan, a mill deliberately blends cotton from multiple bales/sources rather than using a single source uniformly. The primary technical reason is:', '["To make the mixing cheaper regardless of quality consistency","To average out bale-to-bale variation in fiber properties (length, strength, micronaire, color), achieving more consistent spinning performance and yarn quality than any single variable source could provide alone","Blending has no effect on yarn consistency","Only to add visual color variation to the yarn"]'::jsonb, 'To average out bale-to-bale variation in fiber properties (length, strength, micronaire, color), achieving more consistent spinning performance and yarn quality than any single variable source could provide alone', 'Individual cotton bales, even from the ''same'' declared grade, vary somewhat in fiber properties; systematic bale mixing/blending (often using engineered bale management software) statistically averages this variation, giving the spinning process a more consistent raw material feed than relying on any single bale run.', 2, '{"cotton-mixing","blending"}'::text[]),
('9dc3ade3-26ea-41fa-a83f-95a7440889b8', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'A spinning mill notices that switching from a 40-bale mixing plan to a 20-bale mixing plan (fewer bales blended per lay-down) increased yarn quality variation batch-to-batch. This is best explained by:', '["Fewer bales always gives BETTER averaging of raw material variation","Fewer bales in the mixing reduces the statistical averaging effect across bale-to-bale property variation, so the mixing''s average properties fluctuate more between different lay-downs","Bale count in a mixing plan has no relationship to yarn consistency","The mill must have changed cotton varieties, which is the only possible explanation"]'::jsonb, 'Fewer bales in the mixing reduces the statistical averaging effect across bale-to-bale property variation, so the mixing''s average properties fluctuate more between different lay-downs', 'This is the same statistical averaging principle as sliver doubling, applied to raw material blending: more bales in a mixing lay-down means individual bale-to-bale variation gets averaged out more thoroughly, giving more consistent mixing properties lay-down to lay-down; fewer bales means each lay-down''s average is more sensitive to which specific bales happened to be included.', 3, '{"cotton-mixing","statistics"}'::text[]),
('9dc3ade3-26ea-41fa-a83f-95a7440889b8', 'basic', 'multiple_choice', 'Which of these is the correct general sequence of the ring-spinning short-staple process (cotton) from raw fiber to final yarn on cone?', '["Ring frame → Carding → Blow room → Drawing → Winding","Blow room → Carding → (Combing, if used) → Drawing → Roving (Speed frame) → Ring frame → Winding","Winding → Blow room → Ring frame → Carding → Drawing","Drawing → Blow room → Ring frame → Carding → Roving"]'::jsonb, 'Blow room → Carding → (Combing, if used) → Drawing → Roving (Speed frame) → Ring frame → Winding', 'The standard short-staple spinning sequence opens/cleans fiber (blow room), individualizes fiber into sliver (carding), optionally removes short fiber (combing), improves evenness via doubling (drawing), reduces sliver to a spinnable strand with light twist (roving), inserts final twist (ring frame), then transfers to cone with defect clearing (winding).', 1, '{"process-sequence"}'::text[]),
('9dc3ade3-26ea-41fa-a83f-95a7440889b8', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'A spinner is producing a very fine count (e.g., 100s Ne combed) intended for premium shirting. Compared to producing a coarse count (e.g., 10s Ne) from the same raw cotton, the fine-count process will generally require:', '["Lower quality raw fiber requirements and less process control","Higher quality raw fiber (longer, finer, stronger), tighter process control at every stage, and typically lower production speed/efficiency due to the increased end-breakage risk at fine counts","Identical fiber and process requirements regardless of target count","No combing is ever needed for fine counts"]'::jsonb, 'Higher quality raw fiber (longer, finer, stronger), tighter process control at every stage, and typically lower production speed/efficiency due to the increased end-breakage risk at fine counts', 'Finer counts have fewer fibers in the yarn cross-section, making them inherently more prone to breakage from any fiber weakness or process irregularity — so fine-count spinning demands superior raw fiber (long, fine, strong, often combed) and tighter machine settings, generally running at lower speed/efficiency than coarse-count production.', 3, '{"fine-count-spinning"}'::text[]),
('9dc3ade3-26ea-41fa-a83f-95a7440889b8', 'intermediate', 'multiple_choice', 'Autoconer (automatic winding) machines with splicing (instead of knotting) to join yarn ends are preferred in modern mills primarily because splices:', '["Are always visually thicker and weaker than a hand knot","Create a much less visible, more uniform join with strength close to the parent yarn, avoiding the bulky, weak point and periodic fabric faults that hard knots cause","Take significantly longer to make than tying a knot","Are only usable on wool yarn"]'::jsonb, 'Create a much less visible, more uniform join with strength close to the parent yarn, avoiding the bulky, weak point and periodic fabric faults that hard knots cause', 'Splicing (using compressed air or mechanical methods to intermingle and re-twist fiber ends together) produces a join that''s far less bulky and visually disruptive than a traditional knot, while retaining good strength — knots historically caused visible ''knot marks'' and localized weak points in downstream fabric, which splicing largely eliminates.', 2, '{"autoconer","splicing"}'::text[]),
('9dc3ade3-26ea-41fa-a83f-95a7440889b8', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'Package density (how tightly yarn is wound onto a cone during winding) matters for downstream processes mainly because:', '["It has no effect on unwinding at weaving/knitting","Too loose a package can collapse/distort in transport or during high-speed unwinding, while too dense a package can cause unwinding tension problems and increase breakage at the next process (e.g., weaving or knitting)","Package density only affects the cone''s visual appearance","Density only matters for dyeing, not weaving/knitting"]'::jsonb, 'Too loose a package can collapse/distort in transport or during high-speed unwinding, while too dense a package can cause unwinding tension problems and increase breakage at the next process (e.g., weaving or knitting)', 'Winding density must be carefully controlled within a target range: under-wound packages risk deformation and poor unwind stability, while over-dense packages can create excessive/uneven unwinding tension at the next process — both extremes increase end breakage risk downstream.', 3, '{"winding","package-density"}'::text[]),
('9dc3ade3-26ea-41fa-a83f-95a7440889b8', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'A spinning mill''s lab reports that a particular cotton lot has a high ''short fiber content'' (SFC%) despite acceptable average staple length. What is the practical spinning implication?', '["No implication — average length is the only metric that matters","Increased risk of neps, higher waste in combing/carding, more end breakages, and reduced yarn strength/evenness, since the short fiber fraction disproportionately causes these problems even if the average length looks fine","It means the cotton will spin into a STRONGER yarn than low-SFC cotton","High SFC always means the fiber is a synthetic blend"]'::jsonb, 'Increased risk of neps, higher waste in combing/carding, more end breakages, and reduced yarn strength/evenness, since the short fiber fraction disproportionately causes these problems even if the average length looks fine', 'Average staple length can mask a problematic tail of very short fibers; because short fibers are disproportionately responsible for neps, waste generation, and weak points in the yarn, SFC% is tracked as a separate critical fiber-quality metric alongside (not instead of) average length.', 3, '{"short-fiber-content"}'::text[]),
('9dc3ade3-26ea-41fa-a83f-95a7440889b8', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'Why do modern high-speed carding machines typically include multiple licker-in rollers and additional cleaning points compared to older single-licker-in designs?', '["To slow the machine down intentionally","To progressively and gently open/clean the fiber tuft in stages at higher throughput speeds, compensating for the reduced dwell time per fiber that comes with higher production speed, without sacrificing cleaning efficiency","Multiple licker-ins have no functional benefit, just added cost","To add twist to the sliver earlier in the process"]'::jsonb, 'To progressively and gently open/clean the fiber tuft in stages at higher throughput speeds, compensating for the reduced dwell time per fiber that comes with higher production speed, without sacrificing cleaning efficiency', 'As card production speeds increased over generations of machine design, engineers added staged licker-in/cleaning points to maintain adequate opening and trash-removal efficiency despite the shorter time each fiber tuft spends in the machine at higher throughput — a direct engineering response to the speed-vs-quality tradeoff.', 3, '{"carding","machine-design"}'::text[]),
('9dc3ade3-26ea-41fa-a83f-95a7440889b8', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'A mill spinning 100% polyester staple yarn (rather than cotton) still uses essentially the same blow room → card → draw → roving → ring frame sequence. What key process parameter differs most between polyester and cotton spinning on the same machine type?', '["Nothing differs — the exact same settings work identically for any fiber","Settings like roller spacing/gauge, draft distribution, and moisture/static (humidity/antistatic) control differ significantly, since polyester fiber has different length uniformity, surface friction, and static-generation properties than cotton","Polyester requires zero drafting at any stage","Polyester cannot be carded at all, only cotton can"]'::jsonb, 'Settings like roller spacing/gauge, draft distribution, and moisture/static (humidity/antistatic) control differ significantly, since polyester fiber has different length uniformity, surface friction, and static-generation properties than cotton', 'While the general process flow is similar, man-made staple fiber like polyester is engineered with very uniform length (unlike cotton''s natural length distribution) and different surface friction/static behavior, requiring adjusted roller settings, draft profiles, and stricter humidity/anti-static control to spin well on the same basic machine types.', 3, '{"polyester-spinning","cotton-vs-synthetic"}'::text[]),
('9dc3ade3-26ea-41fa-a83f-95a7440889b8', 'intermediate', 'multiple_choice', 'A count-strength product (CSP) or tenacity specification for a yarn order primarily tells the buyer:', '["The yarn''s exact color shade","A standardized measure combining yarn fineness and breaking strength, letting buyers compare yarn quality/suitability independent of just the count number alone","The yarn''s total production date","The specific spinning mill''s location"]'::jsonb, 'A standardized measure combining yarn fineness and breaking strength, letting buyers compare yarn quality/suitability independent of just the count number alone', 'Because breaking strength naturally scales with yarn thickness, raw strength alone isn''t a fair quality comparison across different counts — CSP (or tenacity, strength normalized per unit linear density) lets buyers compare the intrinsic quality of yarns of different counts on a level basis.', 2, '{"CSP","tenacity"}'::text[]),
('9dc3ade3-26ea-41fa-a83f-95a7440889b8', 'intermediate', 'multiple_choice', 'Relative humidity control in a spinning mill (typically maintained higher than ambient, e.g., 55-65%) is critical mainly because:', '["Humidity has no measurable effect on spinning performance","Cotton fiber''s mechanical properties (flexibility, static behavior, fiber-to-fiber friction) are humidity-sensitive; too dry an environment increases static and fiber breakage, while excessive humidity can cause fiber clumping and processing issues","Higher humidity always improves yarn count accuracy directly","Only synthetic fiber spinning requires humidity control, not cotton"]'::jsonb, 'Cotton fiber''s mechanical properties (flexibility, static behavior, fiber-to-fiber friction) are humidity-sensitive; too dry an environment increases static and fiber breakage, while excessive humidity can cause fiber clumping and processing issues', 'Cotton is hygroscopic, and its flexibility/pliability plus static charge behavior change measurably with moisture content — mills maintain controlled humidity ranges because too-dry air makes fiber brittle and static-prone (more breaks, fly), while too-humid air can cause sticking/clumping problems in processing.', 2, '{"humidity-control"}'::text[]),
('9dc3ade3-26ea-41fa-a83f-95a7440889b8', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'In quality auditing, a spinning mill''s ''efficiency%'' (actual production vs theoretical maximum) is heavily influenced by end breakage rate. Why does even a small increase in end breaks per 1000 spindle-hours have an outsized effect on overall mill efficiency?', '["End breaks have no relationship to machine efficiency at all","Each break requires machine downtime for the operator to find, piece up (rejoin), and restart that spindle, and this lost time is multiplied across potentially thousands of spindles running simultaneously, compounding into significant total production loss","Breaks only affect yarn quality grading, never actual output volume","Efficiency is calculated independently of any breakage data"]'::jsonb, 'Each break requires machine downtime for the operator to find, piece up (rejoin), and restart that spindle, and this lost time is multiplied across potentially thousands of spindles running simultaneously, compounding into significant total production loss', 'A ring frame may have 1000+ spindles running simultaneously; every individual end break stops production at just that one spindle until an operator manually repairs it, so even a modest per-spindle break rate compounds into meaningful aggregate lost production time across the whole machine, directly reducing measured efficiency%.', 3, '{"end-breakage","efficiency"}'::text[]),
('9dc3ade3-26ea-41fa-a83f-95a7440889b8', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'A spinner comparing two cotton varieties with identical micronaire and staple length notices Variety A consistently spins with fewer end breaks than Variety B. Assuming all machine settings are identical, the most likely differentiating fiber property is:', '["Fiber color, which has no mechanical relevance","Fiber bundle strength (tenacity) and/or elongation, since these directly affect how well the fiber resists breaking under the tension applied during drafting and spinning","The two varieties'' scientific botanical names printed on the bale tag","Bale packaging material"]'::jsonb, 'Fiber bundle strength (tenacity) and/or elongation, since these directly affect how well the fiber resists breaking under the tension applied during drafting and spinning', 'When micronaire and length are held equal, fiber bundle strength and elongation become the key remaining variables explaining differential end-breakage performance — stronger, more extensible fiber better withstands the mechanical stresses of drafting and spinning tension without breaking.', 3, '{"fiber-strength","end-breakage"}'::text[]),
('9dc3ade3-26ea-41fa-a83f-95a7440889b8', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'A denim mill wants a yarn with a deliberately irregular, ''slub'' or textured appearance (uneven thick/thin sections) rather than a perfectly even yarn. How is this typically achieved?', '["This can only happen as an uncontrolled defect, never intentionally","Using specialized slub attachments/devices on the ring frame (or slub yarn spinning technology) that deliberately and controllably vary the draft/delivery to create programmed thick and thin sections as a designed fashion effect","By using the lowest possible quality raw fiber with no special equipment","By skipping the drawing/doubling process entirely"]'::jsonb, 'Using specialized slub attachments/devices on the ring frame (or slub yarn spinning technology) that deliberately and controllably vary the draft/delivery to create programmed thick and thin sections as a designed fashion effect', 'Fashion slub yarns are produced with purpose-built mechanical or electronic slub devices that deliberately and repeatably vary draft/delivery speed to create controlled thick/thin sections — distinct from an uncontrolled evenness defect, since the pattern is designed and repeatable for a specific fabric aesthetic.', 3, '{"slub-yarn","specialty-yarn"}'::text[]);
INSERT INTO public.qb_questions (subject_id, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, points, tags) VALUES
('9dc3ade3-26ea-41fa-a83f-95a7440889b8', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'Core-spun yarn (e.g., cotton wrapped around a spandex/elastane filament core) is produced by:', '["Simply twisting two finished yarns together after both are fully spun","Feeding a continuous elastane filament through the center of the ring frame''s drafting zone so that drafted cotton fiber wraps around and encloses it during twist insertion, forming a single integrated yarn","Printing a stretch pattern onto a finished cotton yarn","Chemically bonding spandex powder into cotton fiber before carding"]'::jsonb, 'Feeding a continuous elastane filament through the center of the ring frame''s drafting zone so that drafted cotton fiber wraps around and encloses it during twist insertion, forming a single integrated yarn', 'Core-spinning modifies the ring frame to feed a pre-drawn elastane (or other filament) directly into the drafting zone; as the cotton fiber strand is drafted and twisted, it wraps around and fully encloses the core filament, integrating stretch into a single composite yarn rather than a separately-plied structure.', 3, '{"core-spun-yarn"}'::text[]),
('9dc3ade3-26ea-41fa-a83f-95a7440889b8', 'basic', 'multiple_choice', '''Draft'' at any spinning preparatory stage (draw frame, roving, ring frame) refers to:', '["The insertion of twist into the strand","The attenuation (thinning/stretching) of the fiber strand by passing it through rollers running at progressively increasing surface speeds, reducing its linear density","The removal of trash particles","The winding of yarn onto a bobbin"]'::jsonb, 'The attenuation (thinning/stretching) of the fiber strand by passing it through rollers running at progressively increasing surface speeds, reducing its linear density', 'Draft is achieved by feeding a strand into a set of rollers where each successive roller pair rotates faster than the one before, stretching/thinning the strand proportionally to the speed ratio — this is distinct from twisting, which is a separate, later mechanical action.', 1, '{"draft"}'::text[]),
('9dc3ade3-26ea-41fa-a83f-95a7440889b8', 'intermediate', 'multiple_choice', 'A ring frame operator increases the total draft (roller speed ratio) without adjusting roving hank/count to compensate. What is the direct consequence?', '["The final yarn becomes coarser than intended","The final yarn becomes finer (higher count number) than intended, since more attenuation is applied to the same input roving without change","Draft has no effect on final yarn count","The yarn''s color changes"]'::jsonb, 'The final yarn becomes finer (higher count number) than intended, since more attenuation is applied to the same input roving without change', 'Final yarn count is set by the combination of input roving count and total draft applied; increasing draft without compensating the roving feed stretches the same mass of fiber over a greater length, producing a lighter (finer, higher count number) yarn than the target.', 2, '{"draft","yarn-count"}'::text[]),
('9dc3ade3-26ea-41fa-a83f-95a7440889b8', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', '''Roller lapping'' (fiber wrapping around a drafting roller instead of passing through) is a common spinning fault most directly caused by:', '["Correctly set roller pressure and clean roller surfaces","Excess static, humidity issues, sticky contamination (e.g., honeydew on cotton), or incorrect roller settings that disrupt clean fiber release from the roller surface","Using too high quality raw fiber","Only occurring on synthetic fiber, never cotton"]'::jsonb, 'Excess static, humidity issues, sticky contamination (e.g., honeydew on cotton), or incorrect roller settings that disrupt clean fiber release from the roller surface', 'Lapping happens when fiber fails to release cleanly from a roller and instead wraps around it repeatedly; common causes include sticky contamination like honeydew (aphid secretion) on cotton, humidity extremes, static buildup, or improperly set roller pressure/clearance.', 3, '{"roller-lapping","defects"}'::text[]),
('9dc3ade3-26ea-41fa-a83f-95a7440889b8', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', '''Honeydew'' contamination in raw cotton (a sticky sugar secretion from aphids/whiteflies) is a serious spinning concern mainly because it:', '["Has no measurable effect on processing","Causes sticky deposits on rollers and machine parts, leading to lapping, frequent machine stoppages, and processing inefficiency, independent of the fiber''s other quality metrics","Only affects the fiber''s dyeing color, not processing","Improves fiber-to-fiber cohesion in a beneficial way"]'::jsonb, 'Causes sticky deposits on rollers and machine parts, leading to lapping, frequent machine stoppages, and processing inefficiency, independent of the fiber''s other quality metrics', 'Honeydew contamination is a well-known spinnability problem distinct from standard fiber quality metrics (length/strength/micronaire) — the sticky residue causes fiber to adhere to machine rollers and guides, leading to lapping and frequent, costly production stoppages even when the cotton''s other properties look fine.', 3, '{"contamination","honeydew"}'::text[]),
('9dc3ade3-26ea-41fa-a83f-95a7440889b8', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'A spinning mill''s raw material buyer must choose between a slightly cheaper cotton lot with higher trash content versus a pricier, cleaner lot. Beyond the direct fiber cost difference, what hidden cost should factor into this decision?', '["There is no hidden cost — only the purchase price matters","Higher trash content increases blow room/carding waste, machine wear, and processing time/efficiency loss, which can offset or exceed the apparent raw material savings","Higher trash content always improves yarn strength, offsetting the extra cleaning cost","Trash content has no bearing on any downstream process cost"]'::jsonb, 'Higher trash content increases blow room/carding waste, machine wear, and processing time/efficiency loss, which can offset or exceed the apparent raw material savings', 'A full cost comparison must include downstream processing costs: dirtier cotton requires more aggressive/longer cleaning (more waste, more machine wear, potentially lower carding efficiency), so the true total cost of the ''cheaper'' lot can end up higher once these hidden processing costs are accounted for.', 3, '{"raw-material-cost","trash-content"}'::text[]),
('9dc3ade3-26ea-41fa-a83f-95a7440889b8', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'Ring frame spindle speed for a given yarn count is often LIMITED not by the motor''s maximum capability, but by:', '["The color of the yarn being spun","The balloon tension generated on the yarn at high speed, which can cause excessive end breakage if it exceeds the yarn''s strength at that count","The price of electricity","The brand of the traveller"]'::jsonb, 'The balloon tension generated on the yarn at high speed, which can cause excessive end breakage if it exceeds the yarn''s strength at that count', 'As spindle speed increases, the centrifugal ''balloon'' the rotating yarn forms between the guide and traveller generates increasing tension on the yarn; beyond a certain speed for a given count/traveller/yarn-strength combination, this tension exceeds what the yarn can withstand, causing unacceptable end breakage — this tension limit, not raw motor power, is usually the practical speed ceiling.', 3, '{"ring-spinning","balloon-tension"}'::text[]),
('9dc3ade3-26ea-41fa-a83f-95a7440889b8', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'A quality manager is deciding whether a specific yarn defect rate increase is a carding problem or a ring-frame problem. Checking sliver/roving quality data BEFORE the ring frame is a useful diagnostic step because:', '["Upstream quality data is always irrelevant to a downstream defect","If the sliver/roving already shows the same irregularity pattern, the root cause is upstream (carding/drawing), not the ring frame itself, which would just be faithfully reproducing an already-existing input defect","Ring frame defects are always caused by the ring frame itself, never upstream processes","Sliver and roving quality cannot be measured before spinning"]'::jsonb, 'If the sliver/roving already shows the same irregularity pattern, the root cause is upstream (carding/drawing), not the ring frame itself, which would just be faithfully reproducing an already-existing input defect', 'Effective root-cause troubleshooting in spinning requires checking quality at each stage — if an irregularity is already present in the roving feeding the ring frame, then the ring frame is not the origin of the problem (it''s a downstream symptom), directing corrective action back to carding/drawing instead.', 3, '{"troubleshooting","quality-control"}'::text[]);
